### What is this variant about?

This is the same Container With Most Water problem, emphasising the opposite-end two-pointer approach and the greedy correctness proof. The emphasis here is on proving why the greedy move never misses the optimum, which is the core interview question.

```java
package com.bank.dsa;

public class ReservePoolAnalyser {
    public int maxArea(int[] dayBalances) {
        // implementation below
        return 0;
    }
}
```

### How does the opposite-end two-pointer work?

Set `left = 0` and `right = n - 1`. Compute the area as `min(h[left], h[right]) * (right - left)`. Record the maximum. Move the pointer at the shorter line inward. If equal, move either. Repeat until the pointers meet.

```java
package com.bank.dsa;

public class OppositeEndContainer {
    public static int maxArea(int[] h) {
        int left = 0, right = h.length - 1;
        int best = 0;
        while (left < right) {
            int area = Math.min(h[left], h[right]) * (right - left);
            best = Math.max(best, area);
            if (h[left] < h[right]) {
                left++;
            } else {
                right--;
            }
        }
        return best;
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int[] balances = {3, 1, 2, 4, 5, 2, 6};
        System.out.println("Max reserve pool: " + maxArea(balances));
    }
}
```

```text
Max reserve pool: 18
```

### Why is the greedy move correct?

The area is bounded by the shorter line. When we discard the shorter line (move its pointer), every pair that still includes that shorter line as the limiting wall would have a width no larger than the current one (since we started at maximum width). The height is also capped by that same shorter line. So none of those skipped pairs can beat the area we just recorded. This means discarding the shorter line never loses the optimal answer.

```text
Proof sketch:
  Let h[left] <= h[right] (left is shorter)
  For any j where left < j < right:
    pair (left, j): width = j - left < right - left
                    height <= h[left]
                    area < h[left] * (right - left) = current area
  So all pairs using left as the limit are dominated.
  Safe to discard left.
```

### What happens when heights are equal?

When `h[left] == h[right]`, moving either pointer is fine. Any pair strictly between them with one of these walls as the limit has strictly less width and at most the same height, so the area cannot improve. Moving both pointers simultaneously is also safe.

```java
// All correct for equal heights:
if (h[left] <= h[right]) left++;
if (h[left] < h[right]) left++; else right--;
// Moving both:
if (h[left] == h[right]) { left++; right--; }
```

### How does this compare to brute force?

The brute force tests every pair in O(n squared). The two-pointer approach tests only O(n) pairs because each pointer moves at most n times in total. Each step discards pairs that are provably suboptimal.

```java
// Brute force: O(n^2)
for (int i = 0; i < n; i++)
    for (int j = i + 1; j < n; j++)
        best = Math.max(best, Math.min(h[i], h[j]) * (j - i));

// Two pointers: O(n)
while (left < right) { /* compute, move shorter */ }
```

### What about a banking-domain BigDecimal version?

When day balances are BigDecimal amounts, use compareTo for the height comparison and multiply for the area. This keeps the computation exact.

```java
package com.bank.dsa;

import java.math.BigDecimal;

public class ExactReservePool {
    public static BigDecimal maxArea(BigDecimal[] h) {
        int left = 0, right = h.length - 1;
        BigDecimal best = BigDecimal.ZERO;
        while (left < right) {
            BigDecimal minH = h[left].min(h[right]);
            BigDecimal area = minH.multiply(BigDecimal.valueOf(right - left));
            if (area.compareTo(best) > 0) best = area;
            if (h[left].compareTo(h[right]) < 0) left++;
            else right--;
        }
        return best;
    }
}
```

### What are edge cases?

Edge cases include arrays with fewer than two elements (return 0), all elements equal (area = height * (n-1), optimal is the two ends), and a strictly descending array (optimal is the two ends).

```java
maxArea(new int[]{});            // 0
maxArea(new int[]{5});           // 0
maxArea(new int[]{4, 4, 4, 4}); // 12 (4 * 3)
```

### What are common mistakes?

The most common mistake is computing the area as `max(h[left], h[right])` instead of `min`, which gives the height of the taller wall rather than the limiting shorter wall. Another is moving the taller side instead of the shorter side, which can miss the optimal pair.

```java
// Wrong: using max instead of min
int area = Math.max(h[left], h[right]) * (right - left);

// Correct: min is the limiting height
int area = Math.min(h[left], h[right]) * (right - left);
```

### What should a senior engineer know?

A senior engineer states the correctness proof in the interview, not just the mechanics. They explain that discarding the shorter line is safe because all remaining pairs with that line have less width and at most the same height, so none can beat the recorded area. They know this is the canonical greedy opposite-end two-pointer problem.
